diff --git a/docs/listening-party.md b/docs/listening-party.md
index 5bf112d..cb50e1a 100644
--- a/docs/listening-party.md
+++ b/docs/listening-party.md
@@ -4,6 +4,8 @@ A **Listening Party** keeps the playback of a host and one or more guests in syn
 
 Every participant needs a Spotify login, and Spotify in spotatui needs Premium. The party follows Spotify tracks only: while the host plays another source, the room stays open but the guests get nothing new.
 
+The party also has a **shared queue** (Party Queue v2). The host owns it; guests can add tracks and, depending on the room policy, remove or reorder them. The relay does not store the queue: it only routes revisioned snapshots and patches between the host and the guests.
+
 ## Opening the party menu
 
 Press `Ctrl+p` (the `listening_party` key) in the terminal app. The desktop app has a **Listening party** page.
@@ -13,6 +15,7 @@ Press `Ctrl+p` (the `listening_party` key) in the terminal app. The desktop app
 Press `1`, `h` or `Enter` to start a party. spotatui creates a room on the relay and shows a **6-character code**. Share it with your guests.
 
 - Your track, position and play or pause state go to the guests every 2 seconds.
+- `Q` opens the shared queue on the existing Queue screen.
 - `c` switches the control mode (below).
 - `l` ends the party. `Esc` only closes the menu; the party keeps running.
 
@@ -20,12 +23,37 @@ Press `1`, `h` or `Enter` to start a party. spotatui creates a room on the relay
 
 Press `2` or `j`, type the 6-character code, then your display name (up to 32 characters), and press `Enter`. Your playback then follows the host. When your position drifts by more than about 3 seconds, spotatui seeks again, with the network delay taken into account. `l` leaves the party.
 
+## Shared queue
+
+The host is the only writer. Guests send intents; the host validates them and broadcasts the applied operations with a monotonic revision.
+
+- Add a track: search with `/`, select a track and press `z` (`add_item_to_queue`). It is sent to the host instead of the local queue.
+- Open the queue: `Q`. The existing Queue screen shows the shared queue while a party is active.
+- Remove: `x` on the selected row.
+- Reorder: `J` / `K`.
+- Play now: `Enter` (host only).
+- A guest without permission gets a status message and the queue does not change.
+
+Policies:
+
+| `queue_mode` | Guests can add | Guests can remove own | Guests can reorder |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `host_only` | No | No | No |
+| `guests_add` (default) | Yes | No | No |
+| `guests_manage` | Yes | Yes | Yes |
+
+Defaults: 100 tracks, 5 pending tracks per guest, one add every 2 seconds, duplicate URIs rejected. The host can change the policy in `config.yml` (a UI for this is P1).
+
+Only `spotify:track:` URIs are accepted in v1. If the host is on an external Connect device, the queue cannot be authoritative; run the host on spotatui's own device.
+
 ## Control modes
 
 | Mode | Behavior |
 | --- | --- |
 | **Host only** (default) | Only the host controls playback; guests follow. |
-| **Shared control** | The host accepts playback commands from guests. spotatui guests do not send any yet, so this changes nothing for them today. |
+| **Shared control** | The host accepts playback commands from guests: play, pause, next, previous and seek. |
+
+Queue permissions are independent of playback control: a room can let guests add tracks without letting them skip.
 
 ## While a party runs
 
@@ -39,3 +67,12 @@ The default relay is `wss://spotatui-party.spotatui.workers.dev/ws`. To use your
 behavior:
   relay_server_url: "wss://relay.example.com/ws"
 ```
+
+The repository includes a Rust relay in `party-relay/` for LANs and self-hosting, and the Cloudflare Worker relay in `worker-relay/`. Both speak Party Queue v2. The Rust relay listens on `ws://127.0.0.1:8080/ws` by default.
+
+## Limits
+
+- 8 participants per room (host included).
+- 4 KB per WebSocket message; snapshots are chunked by byte budget.
+- The room closes when the host leaves and expires after 5 minutes without activity.
+- The queue lives in memory; it is not persisted after the party ends.
