index 0000000..a378c98
--- /dev/null
+++ b/worker-relay/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,149 @@
+# Spotatui party relay
+
+Cloudflare Worker that relays the Spotatui Listening Party. Connections are
+routed by 6-character room code to a `PartyRoom` Durable Object; IP create/join
+attempts are limited by a second Durable Object.
+
+The relay is **dumb**: it routes and shapes JSON, enforces direction and rate
+limits, and never stores the Party Queue. The host owns the queue and its
+revision.
+
+## Endpoints
+
+`GET /ws` with `Upgrade: websocket`.
+
+| Action | URL |
+| --- | --- |
+| Create | `/ws?action=create&control_mode=host_only&name=manu` |
+| Join | `/ws?action=join&code=ABC123&name=ana` |
+
+`control_mode` is `host_only` (default) or `shared_control`. `name` is optional
+on create (default `Host`) and required on join. A v2 client adds
+`v=2&features=queue`, but the relay does not require those parameters: a v1
+client keeps working unchanged.
+
+## Healthcheck
+
+`GET /healthz` returns `{"ok":true}`. It is used by the CI and deployment smoke tests.
+
+## Protocol v2
+
+`room_created` and `joined_room` carry the additive v2 handshake fields:
+
+```json
+{
+  "type": "room_created",
+  "code": "ABC123",
+  "control_mode": "host_only",
+  "protocol_version": 2,
+  "features": ["queue"],
+  "playback_mode": "follow_host",
+  "queue_revision": 0,
+  "you": { "id": "host", "name": "manu", "role": "host" }
+}
+```
+
+```json
+{
+  "type": "joined_room",
+  "host_name": "manu",
+  "control_mode": "host_only",
+  "protocol_version": 2,
+  "features": ["queue"],
+  "playback_mode": "follow_host",
+  "queue_revision": 0,
+  "you": { "id": "7f3a2c9e-...", "name": "ana", "role": "guest" }
+}
+```
+
+- The host id is always `host`; a guest id is the relay UUID assigned to that
+  session. `added_by` / `from_id` values come from these ids, never from the
+  client.
+- `queue_revision` is always `0` from the relay. It cannot track the queue; it
+  is only a handshake hint. A late joiner must still ask the host for a
+  `queue_snapshot`.
+- `features` is `[]` when `QUEUE_ENABLED=0`; v2 clients then hide the queue and
+  the room behaves as a Listening Party.
+- `guest_joined` and `guest_left` include both `id` and `name`. They are still
+  emitted on join, leave and `set_name`.
+
+### Direction matrix
+
+| Sender | Type | Destination |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Host | `party_config`, `party_members`, `queue_patch` | everyone else |
+| Host | `sync_state`, `heartbeat` | everyone else |
+| Host | `set_control_mode` | everyone (canonicalized to `host_only` / `shared_control`) |
+| Host | `queue_snapshot`, `queue_ack` | everyone else when `to` is absent |
+| Host | `queue_snapshot`, `queue_ack` with `to` | that guest only; unknown ids are dropped |
+| Guest | `queue_command`, `queue_resync` | host |
+| Guest | `playback_command` | host, only in `shared_control` |
+| Either | `set_name` | internal: updates the participant name |
+| Guest | `queue_patch`, `queue_snapshot`, `queue_ack`, `party_*` | dropped |
+| Host | `queue_command`, `queue_resync` | dropped |
+
+For guest-to-host messages the relay overwrites `from` with the session display
+name and adds `from_id` with the relay participant id, discarding whatever the
+client sent in either field. Queue commands are accepted only from guests; the
+relay never validates queue permissions, because the host is the authority.
+
+`queue_command` has a per-session token bucket: burst 10, refill 2/s. Exceeding
+it drops the command and sends `{"type":"error","message":"Rate limited"}`.
+Other limits are unchanged: 4 KiB per frame (configurable), 8 participants,
+1 host, 2 guests per IP, 10 create and 10 join attempts per IP per hour, and a
+5-minute inactivity expiry.
+
+`sync_state`, `playback_command`, `set_control_mode`, `set_name`, `heartbeat`,
+`error` and `room_closed` remain supported; the queue changes only add fields.
+Unknown client types and `queue_*` from the wrong role are ignored without
+closing the socket.
+
+The full wire specification is in
+[`docs/protocolo-party-v2.md`](../../../docs/protocolo-party-v2.md).
+
+## Configuration
+
+| Variable | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `MAX_MESSAGE_BYTES` | `4096` | UTF-8 bytes allowed per frame; values below 512 or above 1 MiB fall back to 4096. |
+| `QUEUE_ENABLED` | `1` | `0` / `false` / `off` / `no` disables `queue_*` routing and advertises `features: []`. |
+
+Both live in `wrangler.toml` under `[vars]` (and `[env.staging.vars]`). The
+default 4096 matches the reference protocol; raising it is possible but clients
+may reject oversized frames.
+
+## Develop, test, deploy
+
+Node 20 can run the typecheck and tests. `wrangler` 4.x requires Node >= 22, so
+on Node 20 do not run `wrangler dev` / `wrangler deploy`; use Node 22 for those.
+`npm ci` on Node 20 completes, but npm prints `EBADENGINE` warnings for
+`wrangler`/`miniflare`; that is expected and does not affect `tsc` or the tests.
+
+```bash
+npm ci
+npx tsc --noEmit          # typecheck, no wrangler needed
+npm test                  # compiles routing.ts + tests to .test-build, node --test
+npm run dev               # needs Node >= 22
+```
+
+Production is the top-level configuration (`spotatui-party`):
+
+```bash
+npm run deploy
+```
+
+Staging is a separate Worker (`spotatui-party-staging`) with its own Durable
+Object namespaces:
+
+```bash
+npm run deploy:staging
+```
+
+Deploy the relay **before** the v2 client. v1 hosts/guests keep working against
+a v2 relay; a v2 client against a v1 relay sees no `protocol_version`/`features`
+and disables the queue.
+
+The first deployment of each environment creates the Durable Object classes
+through the `v1` and `v2` migration tags above. Do not reuse one environment's
+namespace for the other. No account id or route is committed; `wrangler` uses the
+logged-in account and the default `workers.dev` route.
